name: honey-brined confit pork belly
source: https://youtu.be/Td-sOB-cvgQ
yield: one belly — Fallow's "level 3", after Thomas Keller
ingredients:
  - item: pork belly
    note: skin on going in; the skin comes off after the confit
components:
  - name: honey brine
    ingredients:
      - item: water
        note: cold, in a pan
      - item: salt
        note: a 15% brine, per the source — see notes
      - item: honey
      - item: black peppercorns
      - item: garlic
      - item: rosemary
      - item: thyme
      - item: bay leaves
        note: lightly scrunched, no need to strip them down
    steps:
      - herbs, peppercorns, garlic and salt into cold water
      - warm through slightly — just enough to dissolve the salt and honey
      - honey in last, and use the warm water to rinse every drop out of the spoon
      - stir slowly until salt and honey have dissolved
      - no need to boil and cool it down; salt dissolves fine at lukewarm and boiling is wasted energy
      - submerge the belly, aromats over the top
      - 12 hrs
  - name: confit
    ingredients:
      - item: pork fat
        note: lard, enough to hold the belly
    steps:
      - lift the belly out and pick off all the aromats
      - rinse under cold running water 15-20 mins to wash the salt solution off, then pat dry
      - belly into the pan with the lard, and warm very gently to 90°C
      - once the fat is at 90°C, into an oven also at 90°C
      - about 5.5 hrs
  - name: press and set
    steps:
      - lift the belly gently into a container and pour the fat back over
      - weight it — 2kg — and into the fridge overnight, 12 hrs minimum, for a flat even surface
      - peel it out of the set fat
      - trim off the excess fat and reserve it, for roast potatoes or general cooking
      - tidy up the sides
      - remove the skin, and score the fat on top in a crosshatch
  - name: finish
    steps:
      - pan on medium-high, belly in scored side down
      - press down as it goes in to get good contact
      - cook it roughly 80% of the way on that fat side, until deeply coloured
      - into a low oven, 140-150°C, just to warm through
  - name: serving
    steps:
      - apple and sichuan ketchup
      - crispy cabbage
      - baby turnips with pork robert sauce
      - olive oil over everything for shine
notes: |
  Fallow's level 3, their take on Thomas Keller's honey-brined confit belly. Four sessions
  spread over three days — brine 12 hrs, confit 5.5 hrs, press 12 hrs, then finish to order —
  but almost all of that is waiting, and only the last ten minutes are timing-critical.

  The 15% brine is what the video states, and it is strong. That is why the 15-20 minute rinse
  under running water is a real step and not fussiness — you are pulling the surface salt back
  off, and skipping or shortening it gives you something inedible. Keller's published version
  runs a weaker brine over 10 hrs and warns specifically against going longer, so if you are
  improvising the salt concentration, err down and don't extend the time to compensate.

  Confit is not deep frying. At 90°C the fat is a temperature-stable medium that excludes air
  and stops the meat drying out — the fat barely penetrates the meat at all. That is also why
  the oven and the fat both sit at 90°C: you want the whole thing to hold at one temperature
  for five hours, not to cook from a gradient.

  The press is what makes it look like restaurant food. Twelve hours under 2kg compresses the
  layers into a flat even slab you can cut into clean squares, and firms the fat so it can be
  handled at all. Straight out of the confit it is far too soft to cut or sear.

  Chill it properly before you go near a pan, and sear it cold. The whole finish depends on
  the fat being set — a warm belly falls apart under the tongs and renders instead of browning.

  80% of the cooking on the fat side. The scored crosshatch is doing what scoring always does:
  more surface area, more paths for the fat to render out, more crust. Then the low oven is
  only warming it through, not cooking it — it has been cooked for five and a half hours.

  The skin comes off and is worth keeping — it has been held at 90°C in fat for hours, so it
  is fully rendered and soft. Dried hard and blasted it will puff, which is the blanched-and-
  puffed route in the crackling recipe with the tedious simmering and scraping already done.

  Reserve the confit fat. It has pork, honey, garlic and herbs through it, it keeps in the
  fridge, and it is better than anything you can buy for roasting potatoes.

  Verdict in the video: more velvety and more flavourful than the other two, softer, melting,
  with the garlic and herbs from the brine carrying through the whole thing. The difference is
  refinement rather than a different dish.
